
{"id":"b548ab58-bc8e-4fc6-b5a8-db6b343203b0","object":"chat.completion","created":1784822616,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    p = case.get(\\\"payload\\\", {})\\n    m = p.get(\\\"min_cluster_size\\\")\\n    cd = p.get(\\\"clusters\\\", [])\\n    pm = p.get(\\\"parent\\\", {})\\n    if m is None:\\n        raise ValueError\\n    d = {}\\n    for c in cd:\\n        d[c[\\\"cluster_id\\\"]] = c\\n    r = {cid: c for cid, c in d.items() if c.get(\\\"size\\\", 0) >= m}\\n    st = {}\\n    for cid, c in r.items():\\n        b = c[\\\"birth_lambda\\\"]\\n        ex = c.get(\\\"point_exit_lambdas\\\", [])\\n        st[cid] = sum(max(0, e - b) for e in ex)\\n    ch = {cid: [] for cid in r}\\n    pa = {}\\n    for cid in r:\\n        p = pm.get(cid)\\n        if p and p in r:\\n            pa[cid] = p\\n            ch[p].append(cid)\\n        else:\\n            pa[cid] = None\\n    desc = {}\\n    def dfs(n):\\n        s = set(ch[n])\\n        for c in ch[n]:\\n            s.update(dfs(c))\\n        return s\\n    for cid in r:\\n        desc[cid] = dfs(cid)\\n    order = sorted(r.keys(), key=lambda x: (-st[x], x))\\n    sel = []\\n    sel_set = set()\\n    excl = set()\\n    for cid in order:\\n        if cid in excl:\\n            continue\\n        if any(d in sel_set for d in desc[cid]):\\n            continue\\n        sel.append(cid)\\n        sel_set.add(cid)\\n        cur = pa.get(cid)\\n        while cur:\\n            excl.add(cur)\\n            cur = pa.get(cur)\\n    sel_s = sorted(sel)\\n    ret_rows = [{\\\"cluster_id\\\": cid, \\\"stability\\\": st[cid]} for cid in sorted(r)]\\n    return {\\\"retained\\\": ret_rows, \\\"selected\\\": sel_s}\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to produce a JSON object with a single key \"implementation\", whose value is a Python source string defining `solve(case)`. The function must return a JSON-serializable object, not mutate case, use only standard library. The task is about condensed tree stability, similar to HDBSCAN. From fixture: input has clusters with size, birth_lambda, point_exit_lambdas (list of exit lambdas for points, not necessarily all points? but size is given, exit lambdas length may be less than size? In example, clusters have sizes 7,4,2 and exit lambdas lengths 3,3,1. So exit lambdas are for points that exited? Possibly the points that have left the cluster? Need to infer correct semantics.\n\nGiven task description: \"Condensed-tree construction, cluster stability, and flat selection.\" We need to produce a condensed tree: start from root? The hierarchy is given by parent dict. Clusters have birth_lambda (when cluster appears) and each point has exit lambda (when it leaves cluster). The cluster size is given. For each cluster, we have list of exit lambdas for points (maybe only for points that exited? Possibly some points never exit? But exits are listed. We need to compute stability for each cluster: sum over points (exit_lambda - birth_lambda) but only positive differences? The description: \"For each retained cluster sum max(0, exit_lambda - birth_lambda) over its points.\" But we don't have per-point birth_lambda? Each point's birth lambda is the lambda at which it joined the cluster. However in HDBSCAN condensed tree, each point belongs to a leaf cluster at the end? But here we have given clusters with birth_lambda and point_exit_lambdas. Possibly the points are assigned to the cluster at its birth, and exit lambdas are when they leave to a child? This is reminiscent of the condensed tree where points exit from a cluster to a more stable child. But we have only one list of exit lambdas per cluster, not per point birth. Possibly each point's birth is the birth of the cluster? That might be the case: point is born into cluster at its birth, and then later exits at some lambda. So stability for a cluster = sum over points (max(0, exit_lambda - birth_lambda