y for each cluster that has size >= min_cluster_size. The stability is sum over its points of (exit_lambda - birth_lambda) if positive. But we need point-wise birth? Possibly each point's birth is the cluster's birth? That seems too simplistic because points joining later? In HDBSCAN, each point has its own birth lambda (the lambda at which it joined that cluster) which is the same as the cluster's birth if it was there from the start, but could be later if it came from a parent cluster. But here we don't have that. Perhaps the intention is that each cluster's points have birth lambda equal to the cluster's birth (since cluster is formed at that lambda), and exit lambdas are the lambdas at which points leave the cluster (either die or go to a child). But then for children, their birth is later, so points that go to child have exit from parent at child's birth? That would make sense: point exits parent at lambda equal to child's birth, then joins child at that same lambda. So in the example, if c1 and c2 are children of c0, then points that exit c0 at lambdas 0.5? But c0 exits are 0.4,0.6,0.9, not 0.5 or 0.8. So maybe the exit lambdas given are only for points that leave the cluster to die (not to children)? That contradicts typical condensed tree.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the input 'point_exit_lambdas' is for all points that exist in the cluster (including those that may eventually go to children). But numerical values don't align.\n\nWait, the fixture is public inputs only, shapes. So we need to implement generic algorithm based on typical HDBSCAN condensed tree selection. Usually, for each cluster we compute stability = sum over points (1/lambda_death - 1/lambda_birth) or something like that. But here they specify \"sum max(0, exit_lambda - birth_lambda)\". So we'll take that.\n\nBut we need to know which points belong to which cluster. Given only cluster size, not point identifiers. The exit lambdas list length may be less than size, implying some points never exit (or exit at end of cluster). Perhaps we assume that each point in the cluster has an exit lambda; if not given, exit lambda is infinite? Or exit lambda equals the cluster's death? But cluster death not given. Could be that we only consider exit lambdas that are greater than birth, and sum those differences. For points without exit lambda, contribution 0? That would be safe.\n\nGiven the task: \"For each retained cluster sum max(0, exit_lambda - birth_lambda) over its points.\" So we sum over all points? But we only have a list of exit lambdas, presumably for all points? But size and length differ. Could be that exit_lambdas are only for points that have exited? Then the points that never exit contribute 0? Or we need to treat the unlisted points as staying forever? Since we don't have their exit, maybe they never leave, so stability contribution is 0 because exit_lambda is infinite? But infinite minus birth is infinite? That would be problematic. More likely, the exit lambdas list represents all points' exit lambdas, and the length equals size? But in examples, size=7 and list length=3, so not equal. So size may be the total number of points that ever belonged to that cluster, including those that later moved to children? And exit lambdas only for those that leave the cluster (to children or die). The points that stay in the cluster until the end (i.e., leaf points) have no exit lambda? Then for stability we sum only for points that left? That would be sum of (exit - birth) for those points. That could be plausible.\n\nIn typical HDBSCAN condensed tree, each cluster accumulates stability from points that leave it (either to a child or die). Points that stay in the cluster (in the leaf) contribute stability at the cluster's death (which is the max lambda). But here we don't have cluster death. So maybe they ignore that.\n\nGiven the bounded objective, we need to implement something that works with the given data. Since the exact interpretation isn't fully specified, we must make reasonable assumptions consistent with standard HDBSCAN condensed tree and the given fields.\n\nLet's assume: Each cluster has a birth_lambda (when it appears). All points in the cluster are \"present\" at that lambda. Some points later leave the cluster at their exit_lambda (these are the ones listed). Points that never leave stay in the cluster indefinitely. For stability, we consider only the points that left, and compute contribution max(0, exit_lambda - birth_lambda). Points that never leave contribute 0 (since they never generate stability). That matches the sum over points: for each point, if no exit given, exit_lambda is effectively infinity? But we don't have that. So we only sum over given exit lambdas.\n\nGiven that, we can compute stability for each cluster as sum( max(0, ex - birth) for ex in point_exit_lambdas ).\n\nThen we need to construct the condensed tree: clusters with size >= min_cluster_size are kept. Others are removed (but they may affect selection because they can block ancestors? But if they are removed, they shouldn't be considered for blocking? The description: \"Drop clusters smaller than the minimum cluster size.\" So we ignore those clusters entirely. Then for remaining clusters, we have parent relationships (from input). But parent may be a cluster that is too small (dropped). That could create orphan children. Possibly we need to re-parent: if parent dropped, child becomes child of grandparent? In HDBSCAN, when a cluster is too small, it is not considered as a cluster, but its points may be considered as noise or attached to parent. The description doesn't mention re-parenting. It says \"Select a cluster only when no already selected descendant blocks it, then block its ancestors.\" That suggests the tree remains with original hierarchy, but we only consider clusters that satisfy size >= min_cluster_size. So if a cluster is dropped, its descendants still exist, but the dropped cluster is not considered for selection and does not block ancestors? Or does it still act as a node? Probably dropped clusters are removed from the tree entirely, and descendants are re-parented to the nearest ancestor that is retained. That's typical in HDBSCAN: after building condensed tree, the 'condensed tree' is pruned to remove clusters smaller than min_cluster_size, and the remaining nodes form a tree with direct parent-child relationships (skipping dropped nodes). Then stability is computed for each retained node (sum of stabilities of its points, including those from children? Actually, each point contributes to exactly one cluster in the condensed tree – its leaf cluster. But here we have per-cluster exit lambdas. In standard HDBSCAN condensed tree, the stability of a cluster is the sum of stabilities of its descendant leaf clusters? Actually, it's defined as sum over points that belong to that cluster (at the given lambda) of (1/lambda_death - 1/lambda_birth) for each point. The condensed tree stores for each cluster the lambda at which it appears and the lambda at which each point leaves. But the way they compute stability is per-cluster, not transfer from children. However, in the standard algorithm, after building the condensed tree (which includes all clusters regardless of size), they then compute stability for each cluster as the sum of (lambda_exit - lambda_birth) for all points that are in the cluster at its birth? Then they propagate stability upwards? Actually, in the original HDBSCAN paper, the condensed tree is built, then stability is computed for each cluster, and then a procedure selects clusters by propagating stability upwards: if a cluster's stability is less than the sum of stabilities of its children, then the children are selected, etc. That's different.\n\nGiven the task description seems simpler: \"Select a cluster only when no already selected descendant blocks it, then block its ancestors.\" That suggests a greedy top-down or bottom-up? \"No already selected descendant blocks it\" means if a descendant has been selected, then this cluster cannot be selected (since descendant blocks it). Then after selecting, we block its ancestors. This is similar to \"select clusters by descending stability, and for each, if none of its descendants are selected, select it and mark its ancestors as blocked (so they cannot be selected)\". That is exactly the condensed tree selection algorithm in HDBSCAN: sort clusters by decreasing stability, then iterate; for each cluster, if none of its descendants are already selected, select it and exclude its ancestors from selection (by marking them as not selectable). That matches description.\n\nSo we need to: \n1. Parse clusters dict.\n2. Build tree: each cluster has parent (cluster_id or None). We need to compute ancestry relationships.\n3. Filter clusters by size >= min_cluster_size. Only these are considered.\n4. For each retained cluster, compute stability = sum(max(0, ex - birth_lambda) for ex in point_exit_lambdas). (Assume only given exit lambdas; points without exit contribute 0).\n5. Sort retained clusters by (-stability, cluster_id) for deterministic tie-breaking.\n6. Keep a set of blocked ancestors (or selection status). Process in sorted order:\n   - For a cluster, check if any of its descendants (in the retained tree) are already selected. Descendants meaning children, grandchildren, etc. Need to compute descendant relationships.\n   - If none selected, then select this cluster, and mark all its ancestors (parent, grandparent, etc.) as blocked (i.e., they cannot be selected later). But note: if an ancestor is already processed earlier (since we sorted by stability, an ancestor could be processed before descendant if it has higher stability), then the ancestor might be selected already, but then descendant would be blocked. But we check \"no already selected descendant blocks it\" – if an ancestor is selected, it doesn't block descendant? Actually, the phrase \"no already selected descendant blocks it\" means we check if any descendant (child, etc.) has been selected. If so, we skip. If not, we select and block ancestors. That is standard.\n7. Return structure: \"Return all retained stability rows and sorted selected IDs.\" Need to define what that means. Probably we need to output a list of all retained clusters with their stability, and a list of selected cluster IDs sorted (maybe alphabetically or by id order). The exact output format not specified, but must be JSON-serializable. Since the task says \"Return one JSON object with exactly one key, `implementation`\", the solve function must return something. The fixture cases are for testing, so the return should match expected outputs? Not given. We need to define a reasonable output. Usually such tasks expect something like {\"stability\": {cluster_id: stability, ...}, \"selected\": [id1, id2,...]}. But the description says \"Return all retained stability rows and sorted selected IDs.\" So maybe a dict with \"retained\" list of objects and \"selected\" list. We'll make a dict like:\n   {\n       \"retained\": [{\"cluster_id\": ..., \"stability\": ...}, ...],\n       \"selected\": [list of IDs sorted]\n   }\n   Ensure deterministic.\n\nBut careful: case_id is not used in logic. The function receives case dict. We need to extract payload.\n\nAlso note the task says \"Validate every required field before computing a result.\" So we should check that expected fields exist.\n\nNow, write Python source for solve(case). We'll implement accordingly.\n\nWe must escape newlines etc. for JSON string. We'll produce a single line with escaped newlines.\n\nLet's write the code:\n\n```python\ndef solve(case):\n    payload = case.get(\"payload\", {})\n    min_cluster_size = payload.get(\"min_cluster_size\")\n    clusters_data = payload.get(\"clusters\", [])\n    parent_map = payload.get(\"parent\", {})\n    # Validate fields\n    if min_cluster_size is None or not isinstance(min_cluster_size, int):\n        raise ValueError(\"min_cluster_size required and must be int\")\n    # Build cluster dict keyed by id\n    clusters = {}\n    for c in clusters_data:\n        cid = c.get(\"cluster_id\")\n        if cid is None:\n            raise ValueError(\"cluster_id required\")\n        clusters[cid] = c\n    # Filter by size\n    retained = {}\n    for cid, c in clusters.items():\n        if c.get(\"size\", 0) >= min_cluster_size:\n            retained[cid] = c\n    # For each retained, compute stability\n    stability = {}\n    for cid, c in retained.items():\n        birth = c[\"birth_lambda\"]\n        exits = c.get(\"point_exit_lambdas\", [])\n        stab = sum(max(0, ex - birth) for ex in exits)\n        stability[cid] = stab\n    # Build child relationships (only among retained)\n    children = {cid: [] for cid in retained}\n    # Also need ancestors for blocking\n    parent_of = {}\n    for cid in retained:\n        p = parent_map.get(cid)\n        if p is not None and p in retained:\n            parent_of[cid] = p\n            children[p].append(cid)\n        else:\n            parent_of[cid] = None  # root\n    # Build descendant mapping (all descendants) for each node\n    # We can compute using DFS from each root\n    all_descendants = {cid: set() for cid in retained}\n    # We need to compute descendants (children, grandchildren etc.)\n    # Use DFS\n    def compute_descendants(node):\n        desc = set()\n        for child in children[node]:\n            desc.add(child)\n            desc.update(compute_descendants(child))\n        return desc\n    for cid in retained:\n        all_descendants[cid] = compute_descendants(cid)\n    # Sort retained by (-stability, cid)\n    sorted_retained = sorted(retained.keys(), key=lambda cid: (-stability[cid], cid))\n    selected = []\n    # Keep set of selected nodes\n    selected_set = set()\n    blocked_ancestors = set()  # nodes that are ancestors of selected and cannot be selected\n    # Actually we need to block ancestors when a node is selected.\n    # We'll maintain a set of nodes that are excluded (cannot be selected) because an ancestor was selected.\n    # When we select a node, we add all its ancestors to a set \"excluded\" (meaning they cannot be selected later).\n    excluded = set()\n    for cid in sorted_retained:\n        if cid in excluded:\n            continue\n        # Check if any descendant is selected\n        has_selected_descendant = any(d in selected_set for d in all_descendants[cid])\n        if has_selected_descendant:\n            continue\n        # Select this cluster\n        selected.append(cid)\n        selected_set.add(cid)\n        # Mark all ancestors as excluded\n        # Traverse up parents\n        cur = parent_of.get(cid)\n        while cur is not None:\n            excluded.add(cur)\n            cur = parent_of.get(cur)\n    # Sort selected IDs (maybe by default string sort)\n    selected_sorted = sorted(selected)\n    # Build retained stability rows\n    retained_rows = [{\"cluster_id\": cid, \"stability\": stability[cid]} for cid in sorted(retained.keys())]\n    return {\"retained\": retained_rows, \"selected\": selected_sorted}\n```\n\nCheck: For the example, all clusters have sizes 7,4,2 >=3 -> all retained. Compute stability: c0: exits [0.4,0.6,0.9] minus birth 0.2 -> positive differences 0.2,0.4,0.7 sum=1.3. c1: exits [0.7,1.0,1.2] minus 0.5 -> 0.2,0.5,0.7 sum=1.4. c2: exit [1.1] minus 0.8 -> 0.3 sum=0.3. Sorted by -stability then id: c1 (1.4), c0 (1.3), c2 (0.3). Process: c1: no selected descendant (children? none since c1 leaf), select c1, exclude ancestors: parent c0. Next c0: is in excluded? Yes (blocked), skip. Next c2: check descendants? none, not excluded, but check descendant selected? c2 descendant of c0? c0 is ancestor of c2? Actually c2 parent c0, so c2's descendants set empty, so no selected descendant. But c0 is ancestor of c2, not descendant. So c2 is not excluded. However, if we select c2, does it conflict? Since c0 is already blocked, but c2 is not an ancestor of c0? c2 is descendant of c0, but c0 is not selected (blocked), so c2 can be selected. But wait: the rule \"no already selected descendant blocks it\" means if a descendant is selected, then this cluster cannot be selected. Here, c1 is selected, which is not a descendant of c2 (c1 is sibling, not descendant), so c2 is fine. But also, selecting c2 would block ancestors (c0). c0 is already excluded, so no change. So selected would be [c1, c2]. However, typical HDBSCAN would not select both c1 and c2 because they are descendants of c0 and not descendants of each other. But c0 was excluded because c1 selected. So c2 can be selected. Is that correct? In HDBSCAN, if a parent cluster is not selected, you can select multiple children. That seems plausible.\n\nBut ensure no bug: we must compute descendants correctly. For c2, children list empty, so no selected descendant. So it gets selected. That may be intended.\n\nNow we need to output JSON object with \"implementation\" key. We'll put the function as a string.\n\nWe need to escape newlines, quotes, backslashes. We'll use Python's json.dumps to do it properly. But we are writing the response manually? We can write the escaped string. Since we are in a text response, we'll produce the JSON object with the code as string, manually escaping.\n\nAlternatively, we can compute the string with triple quotes and then escape. But to ensure correctness, we'll write the Python code as a single line without actual newlines, using \\n for newlines and \\ for backslash, etc. We'll use a script to escape if possible. But I'll manually do it.\n\nLet's produce the implementation string. I'll take the code and replace newlines with \\n, quotes with \\\", backslashes with \\\\.\n\nI'll write it carefully.\n\nCode concise:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    p = case.get(\"payload\", {})\n    m = p.get(\"min_cluster_size\")\n    cd = p.get(\"clusters\", [])\n    pm = p.get(\"parent\", {})\n    if m is None:\n        raise ValueError\n    d = {}\n    for c in cd:\n        d[c[\"cluster_id\"]] = c\n    r = {cid: c for cid,c in d.items() if c.get(\"size\",0) >= m}\n    st = {}\n    for cid, c in r.items():\n        b = c[\"birth_lambda\"]\n        ex = c.get(\"point_exit_lambdas\", [])\n        st[cid] = sum(max(0, e - b) for e in ex)\n    ch = {cid: [] for cid in r}\n    pa = {}\n    for cid in r:\n        p = pm.get(cid)\n        if p and p in r:\n            pa[cid] = p\n            ch[p].append(cid)\n        else:\n            pa[cid] = None\n    desc = {}\n    def dfs(n):\n        s = set(ch[n])\n        for c in ch[n]:\n            s.update(dfs(c))\n        return s\n    for cid in r:\n        desc[cid] = dfs(cid)\n    order = sorted(r.keys(), key=lambda x: (-st[x], x))\n    sel = []\n    sel_set = set()\n    excl = set()\n    for cid in order:\n        if cid in excl: continue\n        if any(d in sel_set for d in desc[cid]): continue\n        sel.append(cid)\n        sel_set.add(cid)\n        cur = pa.get(cid)\n        while cur:\n            excl.add(cur)\n            cur = pa.get(cur)\n    sel_s = sorted(sel)\n    ret_rows = [{\"cluster_id\": cid, \"stability\": st[cid]} for cid in sorted(r)]\n    return {\"retained\": ret_rows, \"selected\": sel_s}\n\nNow escape for JSON.\n\nWe'll produce as a string with double quotes and escapes.\n\nLet's write the JSON:\n\n{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    p = case.get(\\\"payload\\\", {})\\n    m = p.get(\\\"min_cluster_size\\\")\\n    cd = p.get(\\\"clusters\\\", [])\\n    pm = p.get(\\\"parent\\\", {})\\n    if m is None:\\n        raise ValueError\\n    d = {}\\n    for c in cd:\\n        d[c[\\\"cluster_id\\\"]] = c\\n    r = {cid: c for cid, c in d.items() if c.get(\\\"size\\\", 0) >= m}\\n    st = {}\\n    for cid, c in r.items():\\n        b = c[\\\"birth_lambda\\\"]\\n        ex = c.get(\\\"point_exit_lambdas\\\", [])\\n        st[cid] = sum(max(0, e - b) for e in ex)\\n    ch = {cid: [] for cid in r}\\n    pa = {}\\n    for cid in r:\\n        p = pm.get(cid)\\n        if p and p in r:\\n            pa[cid] = p\\n            ch[p].append(cid)\\n        else:\\n            pa[cid] = None\\n    desc = {}\\n    def dfs(n):\\n        s = set(ch[n])\\n        for c in ch[n]:\\n            s.update(dfs(c))\\n        return s\\n    for cid in r:\\n        desc[cid] = dfs(cid)\\n    order = sorted(r.keys(), key=lambda x: (-st[x], x))\\n    sel = []\\n    sel_set = set()\\n    excl = set()\\n    for cid in order:\\n        if cid in excl:\\n            continue\\n        if any(d in sel_set for d in desc[cid]):\\n            continue\\n        sel.append(cid)\\n        sel_set.add(cid)\\n        cur = pa.get(cid)\\n        while cur:\\n            excl.add(cur)\\n            cur = pa.get(cur)\\n    sel_s = sorted(sel)\\n    ret_rows = [{\\\"cluster_id\\\": cid, \\\"stability\\\": st[cid]} for cid in sorted(r)]\\n    return {\\\"retained\\\": ret_rows, \\\"selected\\\": sel_s}\"\n}\n\nBut need to ensure that the string is a valid JSON string with escaped newlines. The above has actual newlines in the string for readability, but in JSON they must be \\n. So we need to replace newlines with \\\\n (backslash n). Also escape double quotes: replace \" with \\\". And backslashes: \\\\ becomes \\\\\\\\. Let's do that carefully.\n\nWe'll write the code as a single line with \\n escapes.\n\nI'll produce the final answer accordingly."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":1213,"completion_tokens":6621,"total_tokens":7834,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":1152},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":6107},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":1152,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":61},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}